WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.850
Fleet management begins with an authoritative inventory of aircraft, controllers, batteries,

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:08.304
payloads, chargers, accessories, firmware, ownership, location, and status.

00:00:08.304 --> 00:00:12.190
Each asset needs a unique identifier and responsible custodian.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:17.371
Maintenance logs capture inspections, flight hours or cycles where relevant, defects, repairs,

00:00:17.371 --> 00:00:19.962
parts, updates, incidents, and return-to-service approval.

00:00:19.962 --> 00:00:25.575
Records should show the configuration that actually flew, not only the model purchased.

00:00:25.575 --> 00:00:30.324
Batteries need individual tracking because age, cycles, storage, temperature, damage, swelling,

00:00:30.324 --> 00:00:32.483
and voltage behavior affect safety.

00:00:32.483 --> 00:00:38.960
A battery should not inherit an aircraft's general ready status without its own condition review.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.414
Preflight inspections confirm readiness for the specific mission.

00:00:42.414 --> 00:00:46.732
Post-flight inspections look for damage, contamination, heat, unusual sound, impact,

00:00:46.732 --> 00:00:48.027
or performance changes.

00:00:48.027 --> 00:00:51.913
Findings should enter a repair or grounding workflow immediately.

00:00:51.913 --> 00:00:54.504
Firmware and application updates require governance.

00:00:54.504 --> 00:00:57.958
Record versions, review significant changes, test as appropriate,

00:00:57.958 --> 00:01:00.980
and avoid unplanned configuration drift across crews.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:06.162
Updating everything immediately before a critical mission can create its own risk.

00:01:06.162 --> 00:01:08.321
Ground unsafe or uncertain equipment.

00:01:08.321 --> 00:01:13.070
Mission urgency, sunk cost, and spare shortages do not change airworthiness.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:17.388
Lifecycle management should anticipate obsolescence, unsupported software, repair lead time,

00:01:17.388 --> 00:01:19.115
replacement, and secure retirement.

00:01:19.115 --> 00:01:23.433
Maintain an approved configuration baseline for aircraft, controller, application, payload,

00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:24.728
and critical settings.

00:01:24.728 --> 00:01:30.773
A replacement component or reset may look ready while silently changing return behavior, units,

00:01:30.773 --> 00:01:32.500
storage, or warning thresholds.
